When my family found ourselves in a sudden housing crisis right here in Corinth, Mississippi, we did what anyone would do: we looked for help. We turned to Crosswind Ministry at 703 Tate Street, knowing they are a prominent local resource. We went there expecting a baseline level of human dignity, an ear to listen, and a standard process to see if we qualified for the emergency housing navigation funds they receive.
Instead, we were turned away at the door. No application was handed to us. No intake interview was scheduled. We were simply denied help on the spot without a single formal document explaining why.
As a resident of this community, this experience shocked me. It forced me to look closely into how homeless services are supposed to operate in our state. What I discovered is something every family in Alcorn County needs to know: public funds come with public obligations, and turning people away without due process violates federal standards.
The Absolute Right to an Assessment
Crosswind Ministry does not operate in a vacuum. They partner with Mississippi United to End Homelessness (MUTEH) and receive federal grant money intended to provide short-term emergency housing solutions and navigation services. Because they receive these federal funds, they are legally bound by the Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) standards.
Under the mandatory Coordinated Entry System (CES) policy used across Mississippi, anyone experiencing a housing crisis has specific consumer rights:
The Right to a Standardized Application: An agency cannot selectively choose who gets to apply. They must log your information.
The Right to an Intake Interview: Every individual or family is entitled to a formal assessment to determine their level of vulnerability and immediate shelter needs.
The Right to a Written Notice of Denial: If an organization cannot or will not help you, they are required by law to provide a formal, written statement explaining the exact administrative or eligibility reasons for the rejection. This paperwork is vital because it officially opens a 10-day window for you to appeal the decision.
When a ministry turns a family away without an interview or an application, they aren't just being unhelpful—they are bypassing the entire federal regulatory system designed to ensure tax dollars are distributed fairly.
Demanding Transparency for Public Grants
Federal grant money is heavily regulated to prevent misappropriation and fraud. If an organization reports to the government that it is actively managing housing resources for the vulnerable, but systematically blocks families from even entering the system, it raises serious legal questions about program compliance. Public funds belong to the public, and they are allocated for the explicit purpose of keeping families off the streets.
When those doors are closed to people in need without any record of their visit, the community loses visibility into how those resources are being utilized. We have a right to demand transparency, and we have a right to hold local agencies accountable to the agreements they signed when they accepted federal cash.
What To Do If This Happens to You
If you or a family you know has been turned away verbally by a local shelter or housing ministry without an intake process, you do not have to just walk away. Take these immediate steps to protect your rights:
Demand a Written Notice: Explicitly state, "I require a formal, written Notice of Denial stating the reason my application and interview were refused today."
Contact the Statewide Oversight: File a formal grievance directly with Mississippi United to End Homelessness (MUTEH) by calling their main office at (601) 960-0557. Tell them a funded partner is refusing to conduct Coordinated Entry assessments.
Call for Free Legal Aid: Contact the North Mississippi Rural Legal Services (NMRLS) intake hotline at 1-800-898-8731. Their housing attorneys can step in to investigate unlawful evictions, denials, or violations of your consumer rights.
Report to the Federal Government: Submit a report to the HUD Office of Inspector General (OIG) Hotline at 1-800-347-3735 or online at hudoig.gov. Let federal investigators know that an agency is accepting public funding while denying the public access to the required application process.
We must speak out to ensure that the resources meant to protect our community’s most vulnerable families actually reach them. Transparency isn't optional—it's the law.
